[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      5[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      6[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - C: `cd c_src && mkdir -p build && cd build && cmake .. && cmake --build .`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      7[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → `c_src/build/driver <base> <exponent>`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      8[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Rust: `cd translation && cargo build --release`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+      9[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   → `translation/target/release/driver <base> <exponent>`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     10[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - Tests: `cd translation && cargo test` (25 tests, none ignored or skipped)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     11[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     12[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Summary
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The Rust translation as delivered produced **no behavioral mismatches**. It was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m verified against 8057 argument vectors (hand-enumerated branch inputs, an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exhaustive product of 44 interesting argument fragments, and a seeded random
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m sweep) with zero divergence in stdout, stderr or exit status.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two problems *were* found and fixed, both in the verification harness rather
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m than the translation. They are recorded below because a harness that reports a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m false difference — or that misses a real one — is itself a defect.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## H1 — False mismatch: `argv[0]` in the usage message
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** Every `argc != 3` invocation differed:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C: Usage: c_src/build/driver base exponent
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m R: Usage: translation/target/release/driver base exponent
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** Not a translation defect. The C code prints `argv[0]`:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```c
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s base exponent\n", argv[0]);
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The two binaries live at different paths, so a naive comparison reports a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m difference on all 101 wrong-argc vectors even though both programs are correct.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** The harness pins `argv[0]` to the literal `driver` for both children
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m via `std::os::unix::process::CommandExt::arg0`, so the only variable is the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m program's own behavior. With this in place all 101 vectors agree. Confirmed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m independently outside the test suite with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `bash -c 'exec -a driver "$0" "$@"' <path> <args...>`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## H2 — Missed mismatch: no coverage of verbatim argument echoing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** Found by mutation testing, not by the initial suite. Injecting
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `let base_arg = &argv[1].to_ascii_lowercase();` into the Rust program — which
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m corrupts the `%s` echo in both base error messages — left all 22 tests passing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** Every argument in the suite that reached an echoing error path was
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m already lowercase or caseless (`abc`, `2abc`, `1e999`, `0x`), so a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m case-normalising bug was invisible. The uppercase inputs present (`INF`, `NAN`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0X1P4`) all succeed and never reach an echo.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** Added `arguments_are_echoed_verbatim`, covering uppercase invalid input
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`ABC`, `2ABC`, `MiXeDcAsE`, `NaNx`, `0XG`), uppercase range-error spellings
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (`1E999`, `1E-999`, `0X1P9999`) whose original casing must survive into the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m message, interior whitespace (`1 2 3`, `\t A B \n`), quotes (`it's`), and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m format-specifier-looking arguments (`%s%d%n`) that must be echoed as data. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m mutation is now caught.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ---
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviors that would have been mismatches in a naive translation
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The delivered Rust code already handles each of these; they are listed because
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m they are the places where a plausible rewrite diverges, and each now has a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m regression test.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C behavior | What a naive Rust port would do | Test |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `strtod` accepts leading whitespace, `+`/`-`, hex floats (`0x10`, `0X1P4`), `inf`/`infinity`/`nan(...)`, bare `5.` and `.5` | `str::parse::<f64>()` rejects hex floats and `nan(1)`, and rejects leading whitespace | `strtod_accepted_forms` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `strtod` reports leftover characters via `endptr`; total failure leaves `endptr == nptr`, so `""` is silently accepted as `0.0` | Treats `""` as a parse error and exits 1 | `empty_argument_is_accepted_as_zero` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `" "` (whitespace only) consumes nothing, so `*endptr` is the space → *invalid* branch, unlike `""` | Lumps `""` and `" "` together | `empty_argument_is_accepted_as_zero` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `strtod` sets `ERANGE` on overflow **and** on gradual underflow, so `1e-308` and `5e-324` are range errors | Parses them fine and continues | `base_range_error`, `exponent_range_error` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `ERANGE` is checked **before** `*endptr != '\0'`, so `1e999xyz` reports the range error, not invalid input | Checks trailing garbage first | `base_range_error_wins_over_trailing_garbage` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Base is fully validated before the exponent is touched, so `abc def` names only the base | Validates both and reports the exponent, or reports both | `base_is_validated_before_exponent` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `pow`'s `EDOM`/`ERANGE` are libm side effects, not properties of the return value | Inspects `result.is_nan()` / `is_infinite()` instead, which misclassifies `pow(inf, 2)`, `pow(nan, 2)` and `pow(1, nan)` as errors | `pow_domain_error`, `pow_range_error`, `formatting_infinity_and_nan` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `pow(0, -1)` is a pole error that glibc reports through `ERANGE`, not `EDOM` | Assumes divide-by-zero is a domain error | `pow_range_error` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | glibc `%.2f` rounds exact binary halfway values to even: `0.125` → `0.12` | Rounds half away from zero → `0.13` | `formatting_rounding_halfway` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | glibc `%.2f` prints `nan` / `-nan` / `inf` / `-inf` | Rust `{:.2}` prints `NaN` for both NaN signs | `formatting_infinity_and_nan` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `%.2f` on `-0.0` prints `-0.00` | Some formatters drop the sign | `formatting_signed_zero` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `%.2f` near `DBL_MAX` expands to ~309 exact integer digits | Truncates or switches to exponent notation | `formatting_very_large_values` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Error messages echo raw `argv` bytes through `%s`, which need not be UTF-8 | `std::env::args()` panics on invalid UTF-8; `to_string_lossy` substitutes U+FFFD | `non_utf8_arguments` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `argc != 3` (not `< 3`), so 4+ arguments is also a usage error | Accepts extra arguments | `wrong_argument_count` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Neither program calls `setlocale`, so both stay in the `C` locale and `1,5` is invalid regardless of `LC_ALL` | A locale-aware parser would accept `1,5` under `de_DE` | `locale_does_not_change_behavior` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Notes on two things deliberately *not* changed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`f64::powf` vs FFI `pow`.** Replacing the FFI call with `base.powf(exponent)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   did not break any test. That is correct, not a coverage gap: on x86-64 Linux
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `f64::powf` lowers to a call to glibc's `pow` (verified — the binary carries an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   undefined reference to `pow@GLIBC_2.29`) and therefore sets `errno` to 34/33
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   identically. The FFI call is kept because it makes the `errno` dependency
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   explicit rather than relying on that lowering.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m - **`argc == 0`.** The Rust code has a `(null)` fallback for a missing `argv[0]`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   It is unreachable on this kernel: `execve` with an empty `argv` still yields
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `argc == 1` with `argv[0]` pointing at an empty string. Verified with a helper
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   that calls `execve(path, {NULL}, {NULL})` — both programs print
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `Usage:  base exponent`, agreeing.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Harness confidence
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The suite was mutation-tested by injecting 14 deliberate defects into a scratch
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m copy of `translation/` (never the real tree, never `c_src/`). Every
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m behavior-changing mutation was caught, including an exit-status-only change that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m a stdout-only comparison would have missed:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
